  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 314 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An open wave of low pressure passes south of our\nforecast area today, spreading some light rain showers across \nthe region. Best chance for showers in our area will be across \nsouthern Vermont, but have some mention of showers across areas \nas far north as the international border. Maximum temperatures \ntoday will range through the 70s. Rainfall totals will be up to \nfour tenths of an inch in our extreme southern zones, with many \nareas not measuring. A ridge of surface high pressure will then \nbuild into our area from the north on Friday into Friday night. \nThis will bring drier weather, and some gusty winds on Friday \nfollowed by cool temperatures Friday night with ideal \nradiational cooling conditions in place.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Another low pressure system will pass south of \nour area Saturday night into Sunday, once again our best chances\nfor showers will be across our southern zones. Models are \nfinally starting to fall into better agreement with how far \nnorth the precipitation will spread. Precipitation will be more \nwidespread with this system than the previous one. At this time \naround a tenth of an inch of rain along the international border\nup to three quarters of an inch in southern Vermont can be \nexpected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Our region should be under the influence of a \nseasonably cool high pressure area gradually moving eastward to \nstart the work week. Temperatures should be about 10 degrees \nbelow normal Monday trending to around normal on Wednesday. With\nthis pattern and 925 millibar temperatures falling to near the \n10th percentile, Tuesday morning should be another frosty one \nfor sheltered areas that already saw frost Saturday morning. \n\nAn expansive ridge over southern Quebec with the upper level \nridge axis centered farther west will tend to keep our air quite\ndry through this period, with both temperatures and dew points \ngradually modifying. Somewhat increasing spread in guidance with\nregards to the position of a slow moving trough to our south, \nand associated increases in moisture, is evident. As such, \nslight chances of rain do come farther north/east at times \nTuesday and Wednesday, possibly reaching our region. For now, \nthe consensus strongly favors rainfree conditions (PoPs 15% or \nless) even if cloud cover tends to increase.",
